		<dc:title id="title">Brigadier Gerard Stories</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#title">Brigadier Gerard Stories</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-1">Napoleonic Wars, 1800-1815--Fiction</dc:subject>
		<dc:subject id="subject-2">Adventure stories, English</dc:subject>
		<dc:subject id="subject-3">War stories, English</dc:subject>
		<dc:subject id="subject-4">France--History, Military--19th century--Fiction</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-1">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-1">sh85089767</meta>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-2">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-2">sh85001077</meta>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-3">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-3">sh85145222</meta>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-4">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-4">sh85051434</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Adventure</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Fiction</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Shorts</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">A gallant French cavalryman recounts his adventures with the major figures and in the events of the Napoleonic Wars of the 19th Century, in which Napoleon and his Grande Armée faced the mighty empires of Europe.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;When the gallant French cavalryman Brigadier Gerard appeared in the first of the eighteen short stories &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/arthur-conan-doyle"&gt;Arthur Conan Doyle&lt;/a&gt; wrote about him, the reading public was still reeling from the “death” of Conan Doyle’s most famous creation, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/collections/sherlock-holmes"&gt;Sherlock Holmes&lt;/a&gt;, the previous year. What they found in Brigadier Gerard was someone very different from the intense, emotionless consulting detective of their own Victorian Era: a courageous and skilled but vain and emotional soldier-adventurer in search of glory for himself and France, devoted to his elderly mother and the Emperor Napoleon in the extreme, and witness to some of the greatest events in European history as he fought the men and wooed the women of countless countries during the Napoleonic Wars. The Gerard of these stories is aged and retired but recalls and recounts vividly the grand adventures of his youth, weaving romance and adventure, conflict and cunning, and world-changing events of military and political history with the human drama of those caught up in a turbulent era of war. His tales are thrilling ones in which he of course plays the leading role.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;In writing the Brigadier’s tales of his adventures, Conan Doyle brought to bear his own knowledge of Napoleonic history, informed by the insights on warfare he gained during in South Africa during the Boer War. His interest in sport is displayed in those of Gerard’s tales which touch upon boxing, fox-hunting, and cricket. His keen eye as a social observer is evident in a number of stories where the Brigadier’s misperceptions about English society, prejudices from his own French viewpoint, and errors from his limited perspective of some events highlight the idiosyncrasies of English life in both the upper and lower classes during the late Georgian and Regency eras. Modern author George MacDonald Fraser cited Brigadier Gerard’s adventures as a major influence upon his own bestselling &lt;i&gt;Flashman&lt;/i&gt; series.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;In the dozen and a half stories collected here, you will experience the Napoleonic Era from a perspective very unlike that of &lt;i&gt;&lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/leo-tolstoy/war-and-peace/louise-maude_aylmer-maude"&gt;War and Peace&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/i&gt; or other Napoleonic memoirs, histories, or works of fiction.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
